Next_State = signal
Usually, FSMs are written as next_state = constant_value. If next_state = signal,
the signal's value cannot be determined. This kind of statement is created as a transition
to a special bundle node, which means that it may have many indeterminable transitions.
The following example shows this type of FSM:
module sig_control(clock);
parameter

Next_State = signal

S0 = 2'h1,
S1 = 2'h2,
S2 = 2'h3,
S3 = 2'h0;
input clock;
reg [3:0] current_state;
reg reset,enable;
reg [3:0] return;
always @(posedge clock)
if (reset)
return <= S0;
case (current_state)
S0: begin
return <= S1;
if (enable)
current_state <= S3;
current_state <= S0;
end
S1: current_state <= S2;
S2: current_state <= S0;
S3: current_state <= return;
default: current_state<= S0;
end
end

