The details of the above example are not going to be described. The example just shows
how to compile, make, and run the program.

Extern "C" Declaration
Extern "C" determines how symbols in the generated object file should be named. If a
function is declared without extern "C", the symbol name in the object file uses C++ name
mangling.
This might cause some issues when Verdi launches the shared library and executing the
functions.

Therefore, always use the extern "C" declaration for the functions that you like to execute:
extern int my_program_main (int argc, char* argv[]);
}

int my_program_main (int argc, char* argv[])
{

}
In the extern "C" block, multiple functions are allowed.

Main Function is Unnecessary
Since the NPI program is made to a shared library, the "main" function is not allowed.
Instead, you just need to give the main entry function to your VC Apps (for example,
my_program_main() in above example).
When Verdi launches the shared library, it executes your main entry function by the
specific Tcl commands - npiDlOpen and npiDlSym. The Tcl commands is described in
detail later.

Compile and Make the Shared Library
The following command shows how to compile my_program.cpp with NPI. Specify the
path to the NPI header files (npi.h and npi_hdl.h in this example) after -I. Assume the NPI
L0 and NPI L1 libraries are used in the program.
The option "-fPIC" is a must to make the shared library.
%> g++ -fPIC -c my_program.cpp -I$VERDI_HOME/share/NPI/inc -I$VERDI_HOME/
share/NPI/L1/C/inc

The following command shows how to make the shared library "my_program.so".
The option "-shared" is a must to make the shared library. It is unnecessary to
link the libraries like dllib, pthreadlib, zlib, and so on.
%> g++ -shared -o my_program.so my_program.o

