For the C/C++ programming, there is one type of class object - Macro. The object is used
to get detailed information about a macro.

Using NPI Text Model in C/C++ Programming
In this section, a sample showing you how to work with the NPI Text model C/C++
interface is introduced.
You need to include the file "npi.h" in your C/C++ program for the npi_init,
npi_load_design, and npi_end functions. Also, you need to include the file "npi_text.h" in
your C/C++ program for using the NPI Text model.
The sample C++ program finds all words with the npiTextMacroName attribute inside the
current design.
The sample C++ program "find_macros.cpp":

Using NPI Text Model in C/C++ Programming

{
npiTextHandle file_hdl, line_hdl, word_hdl;
npiTextIter file_iter, line_iter, word_iter;
file_iter = npi_text_iter_start ( npiTextFile, NULL );
while ( NULL != ( file_hdl = npi_text_iter_next ( file_iter ) ) ) {
while ( NULL != ( line_hdl = npi_text_iter_next ( line_iter ) ) ) {
while ( NULL != ( word_hdl = npi_text_iter_next ( word_iter ) ) )
{
if ( npiTextMacroName == npi_text_property
( npiTextWordAttribute, word_hdl ) )
{
npiTextMacro macro(word_hdl);
printf("** macro name: %s\n", npi_text_property_str
(npiTextWordName, word_hdl));
printf("** macro is ok: %d\n", macro.ok());
printf("** macro arg num: %d\n", macro.arg_num());
printf("** macro value: %s\n", macro.value());
for(int i=0; i<macro.arg_num(); i++) {
printf("** macro arg (%d): %s\n", i, macro.arg(i));
}
printf("** macro def file: %s\n", macro.def_file());
printf("** macro def line: %d\n", macro.def_line());
printf("** macro def value: %s\n", macro.def_value());
for(int i=0; i<macro.arg_num(); i++) {
printf("** macro def arg (%d): %s\n", i, macro.def_arg(i));
}
printf ("line: %d, %s", npi_text_property
( npiTextLineNumber, line_hdl ),
npi_text_property_str ( npiTextLineContent, line_hdl ) );
}
}
}
}
npi_text_iter_stop ( file_iter );
}

Using NPI Text Model in C/C++ Programming

Where the npi_init, npi_load_design and the npi_end functions are essential to include in
any C/C++ NPI programs:
* npi_init: initializes the NPI setting.
* npi_load_design: loads the design from input files. NPI is unable to get the design
information until npi_load_design is executed.
* npi_end: do resets the NPI setting
The valid NPI program must be enclosed by npi_init and npi_end. The
npi_load_design is only valid when it is placed between npi_init and
npi_end.
You can compile this find_macro.cpp with "npi.h", "npi_text.h", "npi_type.h", and "libNPI.so"
using following command. The libNPI.so is placed in the directory of the NPI library. The
command specifies the path of the NPI library (libNPI.so) after the -L argument. Assume
in this example, the libNPI.so is in the current working directory:
%> g++ -o fmacro -L./ -lNPI -ldl -lpthread -lrt find_macro.cpp
%>

The usage of the executable command is "fmacro [design file]" or "fmacro -f [file list]".
The design file is "define_macro.v":
define TOP2(a,b) a - b
